{number: 98}
{title: Beneath the Cross of Jesus}
{key: C}

{start_of_verse: 1}
Be-[C] neath the cross of [F] Jesus I [C] fain would take my [G7] stand,
The [C] shadow of a [F] mighty rock with-[C] in a [G7] weary [C] land;
A [F] home within the [C] wilderness, a [G7] rest upon the [C] way,
From the [C] burning of the [F] noontide heat, and the [C] burden [G7] of the [C] day.
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: 2}
Up-[C] on that cross of [F] Jesus mine [C] eye at times can [G7] see
The [C] very dying [F] form of One Who [C] suffered [G7] there for [C] me;
And [F] from my stricken [C] heart with tears two [G7] wonders I con-[C] fess:
The [C] wonders of re-[F] deeming love, and [C] my un-[G7] worthi-[C] ness.
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: 3}
I [C] take, O cross, thy [F] shadow for [C] my abiding [G7] place;
I [C] ask no other [F] sunshine than the [C] sunshine [G7] of His [C] face;
Con-[F] tent to let the [C] world go by, to [G7] know no gain nor [C] loss,
My [C] sinful self my [F] only shame, my [C] glory [G7] all the [C] cross.
{end_of_verse}
